# This scenario drives the worker's process-wide file-descriptor table to
# EMFILE on purpose (see nginx.conf's worker_rlimit_nofile and driver.sh),
# which nginx logs as
#   [crit] <pid>#0: accept4() failed (24: Too many open files)
# -- ngx_event_accept.c's own EMFILE/ENFILE branch logs at NGX_LOG_CRIT, since
# the condition is a real resource exhaustion, not a benign client event.
# "accept4()" not "accept()": this repo's CI legs build with NGX_HAVE_ACCEPT4
# (Linux), which prefers accept4() and names it in the log line; a build
# without it would say "accept()" instead (see ngx_event_accept.c's own
# NGX_HAVE_ACCEPT4 branching), so the pattern matches either spelling, same as
# driver.sh's own witness grep. That witness is the EXACT text driver.sh polls
# for, so this is the expected outcome the scenario exists to provoke, not a
# finding; without this exemption prober_scrape_log's [alert|crit|emerg] gate
# would fail the run on the very line the scenario is built to assert.
#
# Scoped to that one message, not the whole crit level: a segfault, a
# double-free or an unrelated crit anywhere else in the same run still fails
# the scrape, which is the entire point of PROBER_ALLOW_LOG being a pattern
# and not a blanket switch. driver.sh's own witness grep asserts this same
# text POSITIVELY, so the two cannot silently drift apart -- exempting a line
# the scenario no longer produces would fail that grep first.
PROBER_ALLOW_LOG='accept4?\(\) failed \(24: Too many open files\)'
